---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/privy-vs-brevo';
const title = 'Privy vs Brevo for Shopify: 2026 Comparison';
const desc = "Compare Privy marketing with Brevo marketing and transactional email for a multilingual Shopify store. A proposed handoff test, costs and ownership criteria.";

const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Which requirements should decide between Privy and Brevo?",
    "answer": "Decide separately on marketing execution, capture, operational API delivery and maintenance. Revenue alone does not select a platform."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can Brevo replace Privy for capture?",
    "answer": "Test the actual form design, targeting and destination fields you need. Do not assume either equivalent capture performance or that two subscriptions are necessary."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should I compare pricing at 10,000 contacts?",
    "answer": "Request current quotes using the same send volumes, selected channels, support and implementation work. A contact count alone is insufficient."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I use them together?",
    "answer": "Only with a demonstrated data route and named message owners. Test preference changes and disable overlapping triggers before enabling the new owner."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I consider Sequenzy?",
    "answer": "Include it if an unmet email requirement warrants another trial. Verify Shopify events and suppression rather than assuming it is a faster graduation path."
  },
  {
    "question": "What does Brevo offer for transactional email?",
    "answer": "Its documentation describes API and SMTP sending. Your Shopify event routing, deduplication and template ownership still need implementation review."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which is better for EU merchants?",
    "answer": "Assess your actual processing, retention, transfer and consent requirements. Vendor location alone cannot settle the decision."
  },
  {
    "question": "What test should decide the architecture?",
    "answer": "Trace a multilingual shipment notice and a separate opted-in campaign end to end. Include repeated events, language changes and a marketing unsubscribe."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-cyan-100/90">/</span>
        <span class="text-cyan-100/90">Privy vs Brevo</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="badge">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Privy vs Brevo for Shopify</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-cyan-100">A multilingual store may need storefront signup, promotional email and operational delivery notices. Those are separate jobs even when one vendor supports several of them. Compare Privy as an email, SMS and list-growth platform with Brevo as a candidate for marketing and transactional delivery. The decision is where each message originates, which data it needs and who maintains it.</p>
        <p class="mt-5 text-cyan-100/90">This is a requirements comparison with proposed exercises, not a hands-on performance study. SMS scope, destination eligibility and support need confirmation for the selected account when used.</p>
      </header>
      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black text-white">Comparison at a glance</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Privy</th><th>Brevo</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Marketing scope</td><td>Email, SMS and list growth</td><td>Marketing plus a documented transactional API/SMTP offering</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Operational notice</td><td>Keep the existing notice owner during evaluation</td><td>Evaluate API delivery separately from campaigns</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Multilingual setup</td><td>Test locale capture and message selection</td><td>Test language mapping across connector and API</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Combined stack</td><td>May own capture or marketing</td><td>May own selected messages; avoid duplicate triggers</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cost inputs</td><td>Quote the selected products and support scope</td><td>Quote marketing, transactional volume and implementation</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Start with message purpose, not a compliance badge</h2>
        <p>Brevo documents transactional sending by API and SMTP. That makes it a candidate for application-generated notices; it does not prove that installing a Shopify connector replaces every store notification. Neither a European company address nor a feature label establishes that your store meets its obligations. Keep privacy, retention and transfer requirements in a separate review with the people responsible for them.</p>
        <h2>Proposed exercise: a delayed international shipment</h2>
        <p>Use a test order for a French-language buyer who has not joined the marketing list. Prepare a shipment-delay notice with order reference, revised estimate and support link. Separately prepare a promotional replenishment email for an opted-in customer. The exercise asks whether the right system can send each message with the right purpose and language; it is not a report of merchant results.</p>
        <h2>Trace the notice from order event to inbox</h2>
        <p>Write down the event producer, order identifier, recipient address, locale and template version. Send the same test event twice and check how your integration prevents a duplicate notice. Change the delivery estimate and verify that the queued message uses the intended value. If deduplication belongs in custom middleware, include its maintenance in the decision rather than crediting it to either platform.</p>
        <h2>Keep marketing and operational preferences distinct</h2>
        <p>Map marketing opt-in, unsubscribe and language preference to named fields. Test a marketing unsubscribe while a shipment notice is waiting. A change in promotional preferences should not silently turn into a language reset or a marketing resubscription. Ask the operator responsible for message classification to approve the notice content; adding an offer to it changes the question being evaluated.</p>
        <h2>Evaluate capture without assuming an automatic handoff</h2>
        <p>Privy lists external connectors, but a catalog entry is not proof that your required fields reach Brevo in the required direction. Demonstrate the actual route before choosing a combined stack. Submit a popup in each supported locale, inspect the destination record, then update the address and repeat. Reject a design that needs manual exports for time-sensitive language or preference updates.</p>
        <h2>Build a useful marketing comparison too</h2>
        <p>Draft the same welcome sequence and cart reminder in both marketing candidates using identical creative. Place a test purchase before the queued reminder, then inspect cancellation and language selection. Record operator work to change the offer in each locale. This tests maintainability without inventing conversion rates or presuming that Privy is only a form builder.</p>
        <h2>Price the architecture rather than a contact-count anecdote</h2>
        <p>Request quotes for your intended products, send volumes, SMS destinations if used, support and connector work. Separate store-notification delivery from promotional volume. In a hypothetical internal estimate, three locale templates taking two hours each plus four hours of integration review total ten hours; at an assumed $50 per hour that is $500 before vendor fees. These are planning inputs, not supplier prices or predicted savings.</p>
        <h2>Choose the smallest workable ownership map</h2>
        <p>Keep Privy as the marketing owner if it passes the campaign and capture requirements with manageable work. Add or select Brevo when the operational API job or other demonstrated requirements justify the extra owner. Document which existing Shopify notification remains active before enabling any replacement. A second sender needs a specific job, a rollback plan and an accountable maintainer.</p>
        <h2>Where Sequenzy belongs in this decision</h2>
        <p>Sequenzy can be an additional email candidate if the marketing requirements remain unmet. Require a demonstration of the exact Shopify events, locale fields and suppression behavior before assigning it flows. There is no evidence here that a third platform improves recovery or reduces setup time; a three-vendor architecture must earn its additional handoffs.</p>
        <h2>Product references and limits</h2>
        <p><a class="link" href="https://www.privy.com/">Privy product scope</a> · <a class="link" href="https://www.privy.com/integrations">Privy connectors</a> · <a class="link" href="https://developers.brevo.com/docs/send-a-transactional-email">Brevo transactional documentation</a>. Product documentation establishes advertised scope, not your account configuration, comparative performance or a guaranteed implementation. Privy also offers expert assistance; confirm the actual work included rather than assuming managed execution.</p>
      </article>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-white">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/brevo-vs-mailchimp">Brevo vs Mailchimp</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/brevo-vs-omnisend">Brevo vs Omnisend</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-privy">Sequenzy vs Privy</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/beauty">Email apps for beauty stores</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Privy vs Brevo FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
